A draft do that to your face ?

1268
01:39:02,101 --> 01:39:04,108
I 'd take a guy with a gun any day .

1269
01:39:08,148 --> 01:39:09,577
You did good .

1270
01:39:10,037 --> 01:39:11,346
No , you did .

1271
01:40:09,492 --> 01:40:12,492
I 'll call Father Gordon and let him know what happened .

1272
01:40:13,397 --> 01:40:14,858
Sounds good .

1273
01:40:39,061 --> 01:40:41,581
The Vatican approved the exorcism .

1274
01:40:41,876 --> 01:40:43,786
Heh . Nice timing .

1275
01:40:45,333 --> 01:40:48,846
And if we can , then he 'd like to meet with us tomorrow .

1276
01:40:49,044 --> 01:40:51,411
There 's a case in Long Island he 'd like to discuss .

1277
01:40:51,572 --> 01:40:52,620
Really ?

1278
01:41:10,676 --> 01:41:13,546
[ MUSIC BOX PLAYING ]

1279
01:47:15,637 --> 01:47:17,546
[ English - US - SDH ]

1280
01:47:20,923 --> 01:47:22,832
[ THUNDER CRASHING ]

1281
01:47:26,331 --> 01:47:28,240
[ BANGING ON DOOR ]

1282
01:47:30,938 --> 01:47:32,848
[ JUDY CRYING ]

1283
01:47:33,851 --> 01:47:35,760
[ BANGING STOPS ]

1284
01:47:36,795 --> 01:47:38,704
[ CHAIR SQUEAKING ]

1285
01:47:52,539 --> 01:47:53,913
Nanna !

1286
01:47:55,290 --> 01:47:57,810
SAFRAN : You know , being scared is like being made to laugh .

1287
01:47:58,011 --> 01:47:59,538
It 's an instinctive reaction .

1288
01:47:59,738 --> 01:48:02,935
When something scary is happening , you just feel it automatically .

1289
01:48:07,642 --> 01:48:08,886
MAN : A mark .

1290
01:48:09,082 --> 01:48:11,765
SAFRAN : Getting James on board this project was a home run for us .

1291
01:48:11,962 --> 01:48:15,257
When the screenplay was ready to go out to directors

1292
01:48:15,418 --> 01:48:17,393
and we were putting together our list

1293
01:48:17,594 --> 01:48:19,831
there was one name that was the top of everybody 's list .

1294
01:48:20,058 --> 01:48:22,873
New Line 's , Warner Bros. , mine . And that was James ' .

1295
01:48:23,098 --> 01:48:26,197
He was the only director that we ever went to on this movie .

1296
01:48:26,459 --> 01:48:29,874
I wanted to do something I haven 't done before

1297
01:48:30,074 --> 01:48:33,522
and that is basically deal into the world of , you know

1298
01:48:33,979 --> 01:48:35,604
telling a biographic story .

1299
01:48:35,803 --> 01:48:37,330
So , what are you guys ?

1300
01:48:37,530 --> 01:48:39,123
Ghost hunters , paranormal researchers .

1301
01:48:39,322 --> 01:48:40,566
- Kooks . ALL : Ha-ha-ha .

1302
01:48:40,762 --> 01:48:44,308
But we prefer to be known simply as Ed and Lorraine Warren .

1303
01:48:44,539 --> 01:48:49,078
The crucial aspect for me was the real-life inspiration

1304
01:48:49,274 --> 01:48:50,551
of Ed and Lorraine Warren .

1305
01:48:50,778 --> 01:48:53,426
WAN : I could pull from a lot of the stories they 've had in the past

1306
01:48:53,595 --> 01:48:57,075
I think they investigated over 10,000 cases in their lifetime .

1307
01:48:57,275 --> 01:49:00,690
And so that means , you know , a lot of great materials to pull from .

1308
01:49:00,890 --> 01:49:03,924
James is able to

1309
01:49:04,603 --> 01:49:06,490
elevate the level of the genre

1310
01:49:07,035 --> 01:49:12,021
to a point where you don 't necessarily need the blood

1311
01:49:12,219 --> 01:49:15,285
you don 't need the guts to scare people .

1312
01:49:15,451 --> 01:49:17,360
[ CRASHING ]

1313
01:49:17,531 --> 01:49:19,025
[ GIRLS GIGGLING ]

1314
01:49:23,610 --> 01:49:26,360
That 's actually drawing him to the room and talking to her .

1315
01:49:26,523 --> 01:49:29,108
His artistry is off the charts .

1316
01:49:29,722 --> 01:49:34,675
You know , his visual sense , his production design sense

1317
01:49:36,379 --> 01:49:40,241
his eye for the camera , he gets the rhythm .

1318
01:49:40,411 --> 01:49:44,273
It 's less about cheap scares , it 's about where do you put the camera

1319
01:49:44,475 --> 01:49:48,217
how do you frame it , how do you use empty spaces to build suspense with ?

1320
01:49:48,411 --> 01:49:51,989
I want it to feel very real . I always , when I create these scenes

1321
01:49:52,218 --> 01:49:55,567
I want to put you , the audience , in there with the family

1322
01:49:55,771 --> 01:50:00,626
so one of the tricks I do a lot of is if a character 's walking down a hallway

1323
01:50:00,858 --> 01:50:04,568
I would always have the camera be behind the character following down the hallway .

1324
01:50:04,762 --> 01:50:06,802
So you 're there with them . You 're walking with them .

1325
01:50:07,003 --> 01:50:08,858
And so you don 't know what 's in front of them .

1326
01:50:09,050 --> 01:50:11,352
Yeah , maybe the camera picked something up .

1327
01:50:12,122 --> 01:50:13,496
Watch out !

1328
01:50:14,202 --> 01:50:15,577
WAN : Cool .

1329
01:50:15,770 --> 01:50:18,389
And you can see it in the actors that they respond to that .

1330
01:50:18,554 --> 01:50:21,075
That they understand what he 's trying to get .

1331
01:50:21,274 --> 01:50:24,689
Not only just performance-wise , but also by moving the camera in certain ways

1332
01:50:24,891 --> 01:50:27,095
to build up the suspense and build up the scares .

1333
01:50:27,259 --> 01:50:30,641
Sometimes in these kind of movies you write too many scares .

1334
01:50:30,843 --> 01:50:32,730
Because you think it 's gotta be scare after scare .

1335
01:50:32,923 --> 01:50:35,606
You can 't do that , you gottajust build , slow it down .

1336
01:50:35,803 --> 01:50:39,316
Actually , that 's one thing that James said , his first read , he goes :

1337
01:50:39,514 --> 01:50:42,737
- " I love all these scares , there 's too many . " - " We gotta pull some of these out . "

1338
01:50:42,811 --> 01:50:45,494
We 're like , " This is a horror movie , James . " And he goes :

1339
01:50:45,691 --> 01:50:48,146
" We have to build the tension . We 're gonna build more tension

1340
01:50:48,250 --> 01:50:50,138
between the scares . " He goes , " Trust me . "

1341
01:50:50,299 --> 01:50:51,313
Yeah , he was spot-on .

1342
01:50:51,482 --> 01:50:55,792
My approach for the scares and the suspense sequences in this film

1343
01:50:55,994 --> 01:50:59,737
are definitely much ... Are based much ... Are much more grounded .

1344
01:50:59,899 --> 01:51:03,761
I tried to put into the film , you know , things that happened to the family .

1345
01:51:03,962 --> 01:51:07,028
You know , things that they would see , that they would experience in this house .

1346
01:51:07,258 --> 01:51:09,527
You know , a rocking chair on its own .

1347
01:51:10,138 --> 01:51:11,731
Like a creaking door .

1348
01:51:11,898 --> 01:51:13,753
[ DOOR CREAKING ]

1349
01:51:13,947 --> 01:51:16,598
Those are classic haunted house horror movie tropes .

1350
01:51:16,827 --> 01:51:20,918
But there 's a reason why they 're there because when they work , they 're so effective .

1351
01:51:21,850 --> 01:51:23,541
Nanna ?

1352
01:51:24,059 --> 01:51:25,553
[ WHEEZING ]

1353
01:51:25,723 --> 01:51:28,090
I 've always said what makes scary movies work

1354
01:51:28,282 --> 01:51:30,738
is not so much the visual

1355
01:51:30,938 --> 01:51:34,037
it 's the sound , it 's what you think you see

1356
01:51:34,234 --> 01:51:37,812
through the sound design that is the most important thing .

1357
01:51:38,010 --> 01:51:41,271
Keep coming around , come around , and then he starts to back away here , and the thud

1358
01:51:41,435 --> 01:51:42,449
as we go along .

1359
01:51:43,003 --> 01:51:44,377
[ THUD ]

1360
01:51:46,779 --> 01:51:47,956
[ THUD ]

1361
01:51:48,378 --> 01:51:49,655
[ GASPS ]

1362
01:51:49,819 --> 01:51:50,931
[ PIGEON COOING ]

1363
01:51:53,115 --> 01:51:54,740
There 's a difference in wanting to scare an audience

1364
01:51:54,938 --> 01:51:56,319
and wanting to trick an audience .

1365
01:51:56,474 --> 01:51:59,289
James is one of those people that would never want to trick an audience .

1366
01:51:59,450 --> 01:52:03,247
It 's not about fooling them into thinking this is something different .

1367
01:52:03,419 --> 01:52:06,485
He loves to scare you , but he 's just not gonna trick you .

1368
01:52:08,667 --> 01:52:09,681
[ SNARLS ]

1369
01:52:09,850 --> 01:52:11,344
[ WOMAN SCREAMING ]

1370
01:52:11,547 --> 01:52:15,638
And to me , why I think

1371
01:52:15,866 --> 01:52:19,608
the haunted house subgenre or the haunting genre is so enduring

1372
01:52:19,802 --> 01:52:23,414
is it takes place in a world that we all understand

1373
01:52:23,611 --> 01:52:25,269
which is it takes place in the home .

1374
01:52:25,499 --> 01:52:27,888
What has been fantastic for me on this film

1375
01:52:28,090 --> 01:52:33,492
for me to realize my vision , we literally built this two-story house inside a stage .

1376
01:52:33,690 --> 01:52:37,170
And that was amazing , you know , just , like , seeing how quickly it came together .

1377
01:52:37,371 --> 01:52:40,088
And how , you know , we would talk about ...

1378
01:52:40,282 --> 01:52:42,388
And how Julie would find ways to accommodate what I ...

1379
01:52:42,619 --> 01:52:44,113
How I wanna shoot my sequences .

1380
01:52:44,314 --> 01:52:47,129
The driving force is I need to achieve what the script had .

1381
01:52:47,290 --> 01:52:50,160
And what James wants , his vision .

1382
01:52:50,362 --> 01:52:53,546
COWAN : We realized very much what we 've been saying about how James frames a shot

1383
01:52:53,595 --> 01:52:55,318
and what he needs out of each scene

1384
01:52:55,515 --> 01:52:58,384
is that doorways had to be in an exact spot to make the scare work .

1385
01:52:58,618 --> 01:53:01,190
And hallways needed to be a certain length and a certain darkness

1386
01:53:01,371 --> 01:53:04,240
to be able to kind of have things come out of them and make them scary .

1387
01:53:07,642 --> 01:53:09,976
TAYLOR : The details were so fantastic , you know ?

1388
01:53:10,330 --> 01:53:14,935
Um , so I just love when a production designer is on that level , I just ...

1389
01:53:15,130 --> 01:53:19,287
I just love when they 've just filled in every little ... They filled the canvas in .

1390
01:53:19,482 --> 01:53:23,476
For me , I take all these stories and I try to filter it through myself .

1391
01:53:23,674 --> 01:53:26,991
I try to think of it through my own head , going , " Okay , what scares me ? "

1392
01:53:27,195 --> 01:53:29,977
Roger !

1393
01:53:30,746 --> 01:53:34,292
When I first read the script , the more I started designing that end sequence , I realized :

1394
01:53:34,490 --> 01:53:38,287
" Wow , that could be a really cool , iconic moment for the film . "

1395
01:53:38,491 --> 01:53:41,393
I kind of didn 't wanna show her face when she was transforming

1396
01:53:41,595 --> 01:53:43,351
into , like , this scary person .

1397
01:53:43,578 --> 01:53:46,448
And so the way to do that was

1398
01:53:46,650 --> 01:53:49,650
we had Lily 's character

1399
01:53:50,075 --> 01:53:53,042
attacking someone , attacking Brad , the cop character

1400
01:53:53,242 --> 01:53:55,414
and biting him in the face .

1401
01:53:55,579 --> 01:53:57,073
[ SCREAMS ]

1402
01:53:57,243 --> 01:53:59,796
And in doing so , that allowed me to muzzle her

1403
01:54:00,027 --> 01:54:03,158
with a sheet across her and basically spent the rest of the exorcism

1404
01:54:03,386 --> 01:54:05,241
with her underneath this white sheet

1405
01:54:05,435 --> 01:54:08,402
you know , wrapped around this white sheet tied to a chair .

1406
01:54:08,571 --> 01:54:10,164
[ GRUNTING ]

1407
01:54:10,331 --> 01:54:13,811
And then , you know , for that moment where she starts coughing and hacking up blood .

1408
01:54:13,979 --> 01:54:15,026
[ COUGHING ]

1409
01:54:15,450 --> 01:54:18,385
You know , she coughs up the blood , but because the sheets are in her face

1410
01:54:18,619 --> 01:54:22,393
the blood sprays onto the inside of the sheet and so it adds this extra

1411
01:54:22,619 --> 01:54:25,336
really sort of gritty layer to it , to what 's going on .

1412
01:54:25,531 --> 01:54:28,530
And I think it just makes it really creepy .

1413
01:54:39,674 --> 01:54:41,584
[ ED SPEAKING IN LATIN ]

1414
01:54:45,210 --> 01:54:48,723
The key is to , you know , make a scary film

1415
01:54:48,922 --> 01:54:52,752
that has a lasting impact and that affects people way after the film is over .

